# Hamstrack root-volume snapshot age check — configuration (HD-262).
# Copy this file to /etc/hamstrack/snapshot.env on the host, mode 0640 root:root.
#
#   install -m 0640 -o root -g root snapshot.env.example /etc/hamstrack/snapshot.env
#
# THE NORMAL CASE IS THAT YOU CHANGE NOTHING IN IT. Every setting below is either already
# correct on an EC2 instance or is a testing seam that has no business in production. The
# volume this check watches is resolved from the instance on EVERY run and there is no
# variable that can name one — see "What is deliberately not here" at the bottom, because
# that absence is the design.
#
# INSTALL IT, DO NOT COPY IT NEXT TO THIS FILE. `ops/` is a synced path
# (ops/deploy/synced-paths.txt) and a synced directory is replaced WHOLESALE by a deploy, so
# an `ops/snapshot/snapshot.env` would be carried to the box. Since HD-222 apply-config.sh
# refuses that outright: any placed file whose basename is `.env`, starts with `.env.`, or
# ENDS IN `.env` aborts the whole apply. A stray copy here therefore fails every deploy run
# from that clone until it is moved. This TEMPLATE ends in `.example` and travels normally;
# the `snapshot.env` it describes never does.
#
# IT IS READ BY ONE PARSER, NOT TWO, and that is the difference from backup.env. systemd
# reads it through `EnvironmentFile=-/etc/hamstrack/snapshot.env` in
# hamstrack-volume-snapshot.service; the SCRIPT DOES NOT SOURCE IT. The consequence to know
# before debugging: a hand run of `/usr/local/bin/hamstrack-volume-snapshot` does NOT see
# this file. Start the check through systemd —
#
#   sudo systemctl start hamstrack-volume-snapshot.service
#
# — which is the only invocation the timer ever uses anyway, or pass the variable on the
# command line. (backup.env is sourced because a wrong value there produces a WRONG BACKUP,
# which needs about sixty lines of probe-then-source machinery to turn into a refusal.
# Nothing here can produce a wrong artefact: the worst a bad value does is a wrong READING,
# which these metrics then report as a failed stage.)
#
# **Save it with UNIX line endings.** A CR at the end of a line becomes part of the value:
# `SNAPSHOT_SOURCE=ebs\r` matches neither branch and the run refuses to start, deliberately,
# with a message that names the CR — because the alternative is a check that looks configured
# and quietly takes neither branch.

# ── Source ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exactly `ebs` or `none`; anything else aborts the run, which then publishes both stage
# gauges as 0 so the refusal is visible in Grafana rather than only in the journal.
#
#   ebs   (default) resolve this instance's root volume from IMDS and read the age of its
#         newest completed snapshot from the EC2 API.
#   none  DELETE the published metrics file and exit 0, logging one line that names this
#         setting. For a box that installed this unit and later moved off EC2. The three
#         alert rules are noDataState: OK, so with the file gone they go dormant rather than
#         firing about a mechanism nobody wants.
#
#         IT DELETES RATHER THAN SIMPLY NOT WRITING, and that is the switch. node-exporter
#         scrapes whatever is in the textfile directory for ever, and this branch is only
#         ever REACHED by a box that has already been publishing — the default is `ebs`. A
#         run that exited without touching the file would FREEZE it: check_timestamp stops
#         advancing, VolumeSnapshotCheckStale fires within 3 h and never clears, and the
#         frozen snapshot age takes the critical VolumeSnapshotStale with it within 30 h.
#
#         SO DOES `systemctl disable --now hamstrack-volume-snapshot.timer`, WHICH DELETES
#         NOTHING: stopping the timer freezes the file in exactly that way. Decommissioning
#         the check means running it once with SNAPSHOT_SOURCE=none (or removing the .prom by
#         hand) BEFORE disabling the timer — docs/ops-prod-hardening.md §6.9.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS `ebs` AND NOT `none`, and the direction is deliberate. With an `off`
# default the likeliest field outcome is: unit installed, this file forgotten, collector
# runs and does nothing, layer 3 unwatched, box looks green — HD-262 reproduced by the
# mechanism built to prevent it. INSTALLING THIS UNIT *IS* THE DECLARATION OF INTENT. A
# self-hoster on bare metal never installs it and nothing ever fires; there is no
# auto-detection, because a probe that stands down quietly makes a production box with
# broken IMDS byte-identical to a machine that is not EC2 at all.
#SNAPSHOT_SOURCE=ebs

# ── Region ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An OVERRIDE ONLY. Leave it unset: the region is read from this instance's own placement
# (IMDS /latest/meta-data/placement/region) and passed as an explicit --region on every
# call. That is not a convenience — the `ec2-snapshot-read` inline policy on role
# hamstrack-ec2 is conditioned on `aws:RequestedRegion`, so a call that inherits a region
# from anywhere else is DENIED. If you set this, it must satisfy that condition.
# An AWS_DEFAULT_REGION leaking in from somewhere is not a supported input and is ignored.
#SNAPSHOT_REGION=eu-north-1

# ── Where the metrics go ───────────────────────────────────────────
# node-exporter's textfile collector directory. The file written there is
# hamstrack_volume_snapshot.prom, rewritten in full on every run, mode 0644 (deliberately
# wider than the umask: node-exporter runs as `nobody` and a metric it cannot read is an
# alert that never fires). If you change this, add the new path to the unit's
# ReadWritePaths= with a drop-in (`systemctl edit hamstrack-volume-snapshot.service`) or the
# run fails with "Read-only file system".
#SNAPSHOT_TEXTFILE_DIR=/var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector

# ── Testing seams — NOT production settings ────────────────────────
# They exist so the collector can be exercised off a production box, the way
# CONFIG_DRIFT_UNIT_DIR exists for the drift comparison. Setting either of these on a real
# instance points the check at something that is not this instance, which is the exact
# failure mode the whole unit exists to detect.
#SNAPSHOT_IMDS_BASE=http://169.254.169.254
#SNAPSHOT_AWS_BIN=aws

# ── What is deliberately not here ──────────────────────────────────
# SNAPSHOT_VOLUME_ID DOES NOT EXIST, and no other way to configure a volume id exists
# either. The volume is resolved from the instance on every run, matching the root device
# name IMDS reports. A pinned id is the same class of stored fact as the `Backup=hamstrack`
# tag that caused this ticket's first outage: correct on the day it is written and silently
# wrong from the moment a volume is replaced. A check that inherits the staleness it exists
# to catch is worse than no check, because it is a green light over the exact failure.
#
# NEITHER ALERT THRESHOLD IS HERE. 30 h for VolumeSnapshotStale and 3 h for
# VolumeSnapshotCheckStale are provisioned in
# observability/grafana/provisioning/alerting/rules.yml, following HostMemoryLow,
# HostSwapInUse and MailDailyVolumeHigh. If 30 h is wrong for your host, change it in that
# file; there is no variable to set.
#
# NO CREDENTIALS. The two IAM actions this check uses — ec2:DescribeVolumes and
# ec2:DescribeSnapshots — arrive with the instance role over IMDS. Both are read-only and
# this unit will never hold a write: a monitor that can create a snapshot can be made to
# delete one.
#
# Full rationale: docs/design/root-volume-snapshot-age-proposal.md, ADR-0037, ADR-0038.
# Operator guide:  docs/ops-prod-hardening.md §6, docs/self-hosting.md#backups
